WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
 Okay, so now we're going to write a web browser again in Python, but it's going to even be

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.160
 shorter than what we did before.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.480
 We did it in 10 lines using sockets.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.800
 Now we're going to do it in four lines with urllib.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:18.480
 So urllib really is just because the idea of opening a connection, sending a get request,

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.080
 sending the new line, retrieving the stuff, breaking the headers out, doing all this stuff.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.200
 That's so common, why not put it in a library to save ourselves some effort?

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.440
 So here's how we do it.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.040
 We're going to read it in, right?

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:33.360
 We're going to import this library.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.000
 So we had to import sockets before, but we're going to import urllib now.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.680
 And so this is really quite simple.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.080
 It's like elegantly simple.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.720
 You say urllib, that's a library, that's part of a module within the library, and

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:47.840
 this is a function.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.960
 So let's call urllopen and then give it the url.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.480
 Now that's a string which it's going to encode automatically for us.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.560
 So it's taking care of all kind of pretty things for us.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.360
 It does the get, it does the encode.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:00.960
 Look back at that previous code.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.200
 That's kind of what urllib is doing for us, okay?

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:09.440
 Now what urllib also does is it makes the connection, encodes the get request, and

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.560
 then it actually retrieves.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.840
 At this moment, it retrieves all the headers and keeps them for you for later.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.520
 You can get the headers, but we're not going to see the headers.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.240
 And it returns to you an object that looks pretty much like a file handle.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.320
 Because you can put this in the foreclause after the end.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:33.000
 Now it's going to read, run that loop one time for every line of this file.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.040
 And so the lines we're going to get back are bytes, and so

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.920
 we have to say decode, it doesn't do that for us automatically.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:41.600
 We are going to have to decode them.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.520
 And that's because we might need to decode them with a particular character set here.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.720
 And then we're going to do our strip, and we're going to just print this out.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:51.240
 So that's like open a file, read through it, and print it.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.720
 This is open a URL, read through, and print it.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:57.880
 And that's as simple as it is, and so that's what happens.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.200
 This is romeo.txt, and it prints out.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.200
 Now the thing to notice is that there are no headers here.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.600
 The headers have been sort of consumed in the URL open.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.880
 Again, there is a way to say, hey, give me my headers.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.720
 But for now, this is just going to eat the headers and keep them, and

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.500
 then you get to read all the data.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.000
 And the loop runs, and this loop runs four times, and out come the four lines.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.560
 You can go ahead and run this one, it's super easy.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.200
 I mean, literally, super easy.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.840
 And if you can do anything you want, I mean, treat it like a file.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.000
 You just have to remember to do the decode bit when you treat it like a file.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.120
 And so we, that code imported, we're going to open it.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.400
 We're going to make a dictionary, we're going to loop through.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:41.160
 We're going to split it.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.160
 We have to add the decode just to make sure because that line is bytes, not string.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:51.080
 And then we're going to go, our words, we're going to go through the line.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.000
 And then each line, we're going to bounce through the words.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.360
 The inner for loop is bouncing through the words, and

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.120
 then we're going to go to the next line.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.400
 And then we make ourselves a dictionary, and we print that dictionary out.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:07.080
 Now this is, this in effect, other than importing this,

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.920
 opening it differently, and doing the decode,

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.760
 this is exactly how we would process a file.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:17.280
 And so by using URLlib, you really sort of reduce the complexity of retrieving and

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.920
 reading network resources to the same complexity of reading and

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.280
 dealing with a file locally on your hard drive, which is kind of pretty.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.760
 So one of the things then we can do is read web pages.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:33.680
 That was a text file, but you can get HTML.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.400
 And so here's how you read a web page.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.160
 And it's the same kind of code.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.080
 We open a URL.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:43.760
 This one happens to have HTML in it.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.080
 And we read through it, and out comes the HTML.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.280
 Remember that the headers are there, but they've been eaten by URL open for us.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.040
 And now we could write a browser that would parse these less than and

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:57.760
 greater than and make links, etc, etc, etc.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:04.960
 So if you can come up with ways to find these links,

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:09.000
 you could actually write a bit of code that would then have a loop that would go

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.120
 up and open a new one.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:11.560
 Pull out the links, open a new one.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:13.640
 Pull out the links, open a new one.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:14.360
 And so you could.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:18.840
 You could make a thing that would retrieve a program that would retrieve a page,

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:21.640
 find the links in the page, and then retrieve those links.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:23.400
 And we'll actually do that before the end of the class.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.840
 And so Python is a very popular language at Google.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.620
 And I wonder if I'm going to, I think it's a pretty safe bet that the first crawler

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:37.200
 that they wrote to crawl the web, to build the index, was Python.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:41.560
 Because literally, that's all it takes to read web pages.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.720
 And pull those web pages into your web crawler database.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:49.360
 So I don't know, are those the first four lines ever written to Google?

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:50.040
 Who knows?

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:56.240
 So the next thing that we'll talk about is how you handle that HTML.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:01.000
 HTML is kind of yucky and nasty, and so it's not as simple as regular expressions.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.920
 Regular expressions might help.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:06.320
 String parsing and split might help, but it's just too crazy.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:09.440
 So we'll talk a little bit about how to use a library to make

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:10.880
 HTML parsing a lot easier.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:20.880
 [MUSIC]

